  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Day to day warming and mostly dry weather are \nexpected through the weekend. \n\nSurface high pressure will move overhead tonight. Mostly clear skies \nand light winds during this time of year will likely result in \npatchy to areas of fog across the western Southern Tier, Finger \nLakes region, and portions of the Black River valley tonight. Cool \ntonight with lows in the 40s to low 50s. \n\nThe surface high will move east of the region Saturday. Warm and dry \nweather is expected. \n\nKey Message 2...A change in the weather pattern next week will bring \nwarmer, more humid conditions along with chances for showers and \nthunderstorms.  \n\nAn upper level ridge will expand eastward across the Ohio Valley and \nGreat Lakes region early next week. The mean 500mb height pattern \nsuggests that the flow may begin as zonal on Sunday then veer more \nnorthwest Monday and Tuesday. Upstream convection may be able to \nreach the forecast area as early as Sunday, with low chances of \nprecipitation across the Niagara Frontier and the Saint Lawrence \nValley. A frontal boundary is expected to move into the region \nMonday through Tuesday and will likely be the focus of additional \nshowers and thunderstorms. Deep moisture and the potential for \nrounds of thunderstorms could result in heavy rainfall for some \nlocations. \n\nWarmer, more humid conditions are likely across the region. The LREF \nmean 850mb temperatures show temperatures in the 90th percentile \nacross western NY Monday and across the entire forecast area \nTuesday. While there remains a high uncertainty regarding cloud \ncover and precipitation, daytime highs are forecast in the upper 70s \nto low 80s Monday to widespread 80s Tuesday.",
